/* ============================================================================
   ClearSky-OMEGA · firestore-capacity.rules
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The capacityAllocations block, and nothing else — same shape as
   firestore-sites.rules and firestore-assets.rules. Paste it into the merged
   rules for the clearsky-portal database; deploying this file on its own
   would replace every other collection's rules with nothing.

   WHERE: immediately after the closing brace of `match /sites/{siteId}` and
   before `match /omega_orgs/{orgId}`. It sits next to /sites because it is
   the same tenant, the same reps and the same sensitivity class.

   ⚠ AN EARLIER DRAFT OF THIS WAS A STANDALONE FILE THAT REDEFINED
   signedIn(), userOrg(), isOmegaStaff() and canActInOrg(). All four already
   exist in the merged rules with DIFFERENT MEANINGS. Pasting it whole is a
   deploy error (duplicate definitions); deleting the duplicates leaves these
   rules calling the merged file's versions, which is worse, because it
   deploys cleanly and quietly grants role-granted reps on any domain
   cross-org access to every tenant's capacity claims.

   THIS VERSION DEFINES NO TOP-LEVEL HELPERS AT ALL. Every helper below is
   scoped inside the match block, exactly as hasStatus() and mineHere() are
   inside /sites. It calls only userOrg(), ownsDemoBucket() and isAdmin(),
   which already exist and are not touched.
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   WHAT IT HOLDS
   One document per claim on a distribution circuit. Written by
   omega-capacity-ledger.js from the Site Finder; read by every rep in the
   org so that two of them cannot sell the same wire twice.

   Doc id = orgId + '__' + feederId + '__' + siteId, built by
   OmegaLedger.allocId(). Same shape as siteDocId() on /sites, and for the
   same reason: a feeder id is unique to a utility, not to a database shared
   by eight tenants.

   Body:
     orgId       tenant scope — required, enforced below
     id          feederId + '__' + siteId (the doc id minus the org prefix)
     feederId    the circuit. Immutable after creation.
     siteId      the parcel or listing id. Immutable after creation.
     address     for the do-not-call warning on neighbouring sites
     lat, lon
     kw, kwh     how much of the circuit this claim consumes
     status      prospect | reserved | proposal | application
                 | approved | energized | lost
     rep, note
     createdAt, updatedAt
     expiresAt   soft holds only. Absent on firm claims by design.

   ── SCOPING: DELIBERATELY THE SAME AS /sites ─────────────────────────────
   Own org, demo bucket, isAdmin(). Nothing else.

   NOT isConsoleViewer(). A claim carries a site address and the name of the
   rep working it. sunesol.com and ogisolar.com already read /projects across
   orgs; they must not also read another tenant's pipeline. Same reasoning
   that keeps them out of /sites, /intake_projects and /equipment.

   NOT isOmegaStaff(). This is the one that matters, because isOmegaStaff()
   here means isAdmin() OR any active rep on any domain — wider than it looks
   from the name. A rep works the intake queue; there is no workflow in which
   they need another tenant's circuit reservations. /sites makes exactly this
   call and this follows it.

   NOT isPortalAdmin(). An outside partner company has no business reading
   which circuits a tenant has locked up.

   ── WHAT THE RULES CAN AND CANNOT ENFORCE ────────────────────────────────
   The oversell check — "is there enough left on this circuit" — lives in
   omega-capacity-ledger.js, in the browser. Firestore rules CANNOT sum a
   query, so that arithmetic cannot be reproduced here at any price. What is
   enforced below is everything else: tenant isolation, id shape, an immutable
   circuit and site, a status from the known list, sane kW, and a ceiling on
   how long a soft hold can park capacity.

   A patched client can therefore still oversell a circuit. It cannot reach
   another tenant, move a claim between circuits, or hold capacity forever.
   If the arithmetic has to bind at the database — worth it the day a
   disputed claim costs a deposit — that needs a Cloud Function writing
   through a per-feeder rollup document in a transaction. See open question 2
   in README-sitefinder.md.
   ========================================================================== */

    match /capacityAllocations/{allocId} {

      /* An existing row this caller may work. resource is null on create, so
         this is used on read / update / delete only. Same shape as
         mineHere() in /sites — one definition, three verbs, so they cannot
         drift apart. */
      function mineHere() {
        return signedIn()
          && (resource.data.orgId == userOrg()
              || ownsDemoBucket(resource.data.orgId)
              || isAdmin());
      }

      /* The caller may write into this org at all. */
      function canWriteOrg(o) {
        return signedIn()
          && (o == userOrg() || ownsDemoBucket(o) || isAdmin());
      }

      /* The document id is pinned to its own contents, so one tenant cannot
         write a row that another tenant's org-scoped query would return, and
         two sites cannot collide on one claim.

         The client builds this with a global regex replace in allocId(); the
         same substitution is repeated here rather than trusted. Rules'
         String.replace() replaces every match, matching the /g in the client. */
      function idIsWellFormed() {
        return allocId == request.resource.data.orgId + '__'
             + request.resource.data.feederId.replace('[^A-Za-z0-9_-]', '_') + '__'
             + request.resource.data.siteId.replace('[^A-Za-z0-9_-]', '_');
      }

      /* Status IS enumerated here, unlike /sites — and the difference is
         deliberate. On /sites an unknown status renders as 'identified' and
         the row still works, so refusing it would have been a silent save
         failure for no gain. Here the status decides WHETHER THE CLAIM
         CONSUMES CAPACITY: 'prospect' and 'lost' hold nothing, everything
         else holds. A value outside this list would be read by
         OmegaLedger.statusOf() as 'prospect' and quietly free a circuit that
         somebody believes they have locked. That is a wrong number in front
         of a customer, so it is refused at the database.

         KEEP THIS LIST IN STEP WITH L.STATUS in omega-capacity-ledger.js. */
      function statusKnown(d) {
        return d.get('status', '') in ['prospect', 'reserved', 'proposal',
                                       'application', 'approved', 'energized', 'lost'];
      }

      function shapeOk(d) {
        return d.orgId is string    && d.orgId.size() > 0
            && d.feederId is string && d.feederId.size() > 0 && d.feederId.size() < 64
            && d.siteId is string   && d.siteId.size() > 0   && d.siteId.size() < 64
            && d.kw is number       && d.kw >= 0 && d.kw <= 500000
            && statusKnown(d)
            && d.get('address', '') is string && d.get('address', '').size() < 240
            && d.get('note', '') is string    && d.get('note', '').size() < 2000
            && d.get('rep', '') is string     && d.get('rep', '').size() < 160;
      }

      /* A soft hold with no expiry is not a hold, it is an outage — the rep
         leaves, nobody remembers, and the circuit is dead stock forever. The
         client writes 30 days; this refuses anything past 90, so a patched
         client cannot park a circuit indefinitely. Firm claims
         (application / approved / energized) carry no expiry by design and
         are not tested. */
      function expiryOk(d) {
        return !(d.get('status', '') in ['reserved', 'proposal'])
            || (d.get('expiresAt', 0) is number
                && d.get('expiresAt', 0) > 0
                && d.get('expiresAt', 0) <= request.time.toMillis() + 7776000000);
      }

      /* ── THE CLAIM ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         On create the rep must be the caller's own address, or blank. Same
         pin as repEmail on /sites and authorEmail on /team_messages: you may
         claim a circuit for yourself, or leave it unattributed, but you may
         not file one in a colleague's name.

         .lower() on both sides for the reason spelled out in userOrg() — a
         token arriving as Thomas@Chileasing.com would otherwise fail a
         case-sensitive comparison and block ONE rep while the rest work. */
      function repIsOwnOrBlank() {
        return request.resource.data.get('rep', '') == ''
            || request.resource.data.get('rep', '').lower()
                 == request.auth.token.email.lower();
      }

      /* Everyone in the org reads every claim in the org, and that is the
         entire point of the collection. A lock nobody else can see stops
         nobody from selling over it. */
      allow read: if mineHere();

      allow create: if canWriteOrg(request.resource.data.orgId)
                    && idIsWellFormed()
                    && shapeOk(request.resource.data)
                    && expiryOk(request.resource.data)
                    && (repIsOwnOrBlank() || isAdmin());

      /* orgId, feederId and siteId are all immutable. Letting a claim change
         circuits after the fact would silently free capacity on one wire and
         consume it on another with no record that it happened — and the
         history is the only thing that settles it when two reps both believe
         they had the circuit first.

         The rep is NOT pinned on update, deliberately, matching /sites.
         Handing a site to a colleague who has the relationship is ordinary
         floor work, and routing it through an administrator would mean reps
         work around the tool rather than in it. Honest by visibility, not by
         enforcement: everyone can see who holds what and who changed it.

         ← LOCK ATTRIBUTION applies here too. The day the circuit ledger
         drives commission, uncomment the clause below and reassignment
         becomes an administrator act, exactly as marked on /sites. */
      allow update: if mineHere()
                    && request.resource.data.orgId    == resource.data.orgId
                    && request.resource.data.feederId == resource.data.feederId
                    && request.resource.data.siteId   == resource.data.siteId
                    && shapeOk(request.resource.data)
                    && expiryOk(request.resource.data)
                    /* ← LOCK ATTRIBUTION — uncomment with the matching clause on /sites
                    && (isAdmin()
                        || resource.data.get('rep', '') == ''
                        || request.resource.data.get('rep', '') == resource.data.get('rep', ''))
                    */
                    ;

      /* ── DELETE IS ADMIN-ONLY, AND THIS DIFFERS FROM /sites ON PURPOSE ──
         A /sites row is a working sales record and a rep can bin their own.
         A capacity claim is a LOCK on a shared physical resource, and the
         record of who held what and when is what settles a dispute over a
         circuit two people both worked.

         Releasing therefore sets status to 'lost', which returns the capacity
         to the circuit immediately and leaves the row. Nothing in the Site
         Finder calls delete at all; this exists so a test row can be cleared
         without opening the Firebase console. */
      allow delete: if isAdmin();
    }
